<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Nile Delta</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nile">Nile</a> Delta is the broad, fertile region of northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> where the Nile divides into branches before reaching the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mediterranean">Mediterranean</a>. It has been one of the most productive agricultural areas in the world. Its surplus provided the financial foundation for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saladin">Saladin</a>'s campaigns and the court of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ayyubid-empire">Ayyubid Empire</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/nile-delta.jpg" alt="Nile Delta" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: NASA · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Nile_delta_landsat_false_color.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>The Nile Delta is formed in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lower-egypt">Lower Egypt</a> where the Nile spreads out and drains into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mediterranean">Mediterranean Sea</a>. It runs from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexandria">Alexandria</a> in the west to Port Said in the east, covering some 240 kilometres of coastline, and is about 160 kilometres long from north to south, beginning at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cairo">Cairo</a>. It is called an arcuate delta, since seen from above it resembles a triangle or a flower. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aristotle">Aristotle</a> speculated that it had been built up for agriculture, as the region of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> dried.</p><p>Today the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nile">Nile</a> divides into two main distributaries, the Damietta and the Rosetta, which reach the sea at port cities of the same names. Ptolemy and other ancient writers recorded seven branches, from the Pelusiac in the east to the Canopic in the west. Modern Egyptologists think that five main branches existed at one time in the Pharaonic era. Three of them have dried up over the centuries through flood control, silting and changing relief, and only the Damietta and the Rosetta survive.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>People have lived in the delta for thousands of years, and it has been intensively farmed for at least the past five thousand. It was a major part of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lower-egypt">Lower Egypt</a>, and many archaeological sites lie in and around it, among them <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/avaris">Avaris</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bubastis">Bubastis</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mendes">Mendes</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sais">Sais</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tanis">Tanis</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pelusium">Pelusium</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rosetta-stone">Rosetta Stone</a> was found in the delta in 1799 in the port city of Rosetta. The sunken city of Heracleion, off the coast, has yielded a small Greek temple, granite columns and bronze coins from the reign of Ptolemy II.</p><p>The Butic Canal was an ancient east-west watercourse of the delta, with a reconstructed length of about 185 to 200 kilometres, which would make it the longest canal in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>. The earliest, indirect, reference is in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/josephus">Flavius Josephus</a>, in events around 70 CE. The geographer Ptolemy of the 2nd century CE described it as spanning the whole width of the delta. According to Schiestl, the eastern part is much better attested and probably much older, while the western part was completed only in Roman times.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>The delta is a rich agricultural region, with topsoil as much as 21 metres deep. It once flooded each year, but this ended after the Aswan Dam was completed in 1970. The delta no longer receives nutrients and sediments from upstream, so its soils have become poorer and large amounts of fertiliser are used. About 70 million people live in the delta region, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cairo">Cairo</a> is its largest city. Other large cities include <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexandria">Alexandria</a>, Port Said, Mansura, Tanta and Zagazig. The outer edges of the delta are eroding, and its low-lying land is vulnerable to rising sea level and subsidence.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Nile Delta” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Egypt's agricultural surplus came from the fertile <b>Nile Delta</b> and the irrigation networks of the Nile valley.</blockquote><a href="/empire/ayyubid-empire">Ayyubid Empire · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1799 French soldiers found the Rosetta Stone near the town of Rosetta in the <b>Nile Delta</b>.</blockquote><a href="/language/ancient-egyptian">Ancient Egyptian · Change &amp; Descendants</a></li><li><blockquote>Greek letters were first used for Egyptian in transcriptions of names, most from the Ptolemaic Kingdom, after Alexander the Great’s conquest had spread Greek–Egyptian bilingualism, above all in the <b>Nile Delta</b>.</blockquote><a href="/language/coptic">Coptic · Origins</a></li><li><blockquote>Wall paintings in Minoan style have been found far from Crete, at Tel Kabri in present-day Israel and at Tell el-Dab’a, the ancient Avaris in the <b>Nile Delta</b>, where they include scenes of young athletes leaping over bulls.</blockquote><a href="/culture/minoan-civilisation">Minoan civilisation · At a glance</a></li><li><blockquote>Many fled to the towns; others revolted, like the Christian Copts of the <b>Nile Delta</b> in 831–832, after which many of their women and children were sold as slaves.</blockquote><a href="/societies/abbasid-caliphate">Social Hierarchy of the Abbasid Caliphate</a></li><li><blockquote>Their craft demanded fine Arabic, accounting and a mastery of forms, and their manuals show the reach of the bureaucracy: al-Qalqashandi, a clerk of the chancery from the <b>Nile Delta</b>, completed his fourteen-volume manual, the Subh al-A’sha, in 1412, building on the work of the administrator al-Umari.</blockquote><a href="/societies/mamluk-sultanate">Social Hierarchy of the Mamluk Sultanate</a></li></ul></section>
